; The fixtype of source texts and the result fixtypes.
; These come before the controlled configuration below
; because the proofs generated by fty::defresult
; rely on the default theory.

(fty::defprod source-text
  :parents (input-format)
  :short "Fixtype of source texts."
  :long
  (xdoc::topstring
   (xdoc::p
    "A source text is the content of a source file
     as a list of code points,
     without the byte order mark that the file may start with,
     and with a line feed in place of
     each carriage return immediately followed by a line feed.
     This is the text in which the positions of spans count;
     the lexer reads it from after the shebang, if it starts with one
     (see @(tsee shebang-length)).")
   (xdoc::p
    "A source text also records what was removed from the file's content:
     whether there was a byte order mark,
     and where the removed carriage returns were.
     The latter are the positions, in the text and in increasing order,
     of the line feeds that had a carriage return before them;
     positions start at 0.
     With these, the bytes of the file can be reproduced
     (see @(tsee source-text-to-bytes))."))
  ((text nat-list)
   (bom bool)
   (crs nat-list))
  :pred source-textp)

(fty::defresult source-text-result
  :parents (input-format)
  :short "Fixtype of errors and source texts."
  :ok source-text
  :pred source-text-resultp)

(fty::defresult byte-list-result
  :parents (input-format)
  :short "Fixtype of errors and lists of bytes."
  :ok byte-list
  :pred byte-list-resultp)

; See lexer.lisp for why :no-function nil (fty::reserrf uses __function__).
(acl2::controlled-configuration :no-function nil)

; These rules bridge the fty::defresult idiom to the underlying types,
; as in lexer.lisp and tokenizer.lisp.
(local (in-theory (enable acl2::nat-listp-when-result-not-error
                          source-textp-when-result-not-error
                          byte-listp-when-result-not-error)))

;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(defxdoc+ input-format
  :parents (syntax-for-tools)
  :short "Rust input format: from the bytes of a source file
          to the text that the lexer reads."
  :long
  (xdoc::topstring
   (xdoc::p
    "A Rust source file is a sequence of bytes.
     Four steps lead from it to the code points that the lexer reads
     ["
    (xdoc::ahref
     "https://doc.rust-lang.org/1.87.0/reference/input-format.html"
     "Reference: Input format")
    "]:")
   (xdoc::ol
    (xdoc::li
     "The bytes are decoded as UTF-8.
      It is an error if they are not valid UTF-8.")
    (xdoc::li
     "If the first code point is U+FEFF, the byte order mark,
      it is removed.")
    (xdoc::li
     "Each carriage return (CR) immediately followed by a line feed (LF)
      is replaced with the line feed alone.")
    (xdoc::li
     "If the text starts with a shebang,
      that is a first line like @('#!/usr/bin/env rustx'),
      the lexer does not read it."))
   (xdoc::p
    "The lexical grammar (see @(see grammar)) is about
     the code points that result from these steps;
     the steps themselves are outside the grammar.
     The functions here carry them out, as rustc does.")
   (xdoc::p
    "The result of the first three steps is a @(tsee source-text),
     obtained with @(tsee source-text-from-bytes)
     or, from a file, with @(tsee source-text-from-file).
     A source text records what the second and third steps removed,
     so that nothing of the file is lost:
     @(tsee source-text-to-bytes) gives back the bytes of the file,
     as a theorem there says.")
   (xdoc::p
    "The fourth step does not change the text.
     A shebang stays in the source text,
     and @(tsee shebang-length) says how long it is;
     @(tsee tokenize-source-text) starts lexing after it.
     This is also how rustc works:
     its positions are offsets into the text of the file
     after the first three steps,
     and its lexer starts after the shebang.
     A shebang does not contain a line feed,
     so the tokens after it are in the lines
     that they are in when the file is shown.")
   (xdoc::p
    "@(tsee tokenize-rust-from-bytes) and @(tsee tokenize-rust-from-file)
     go from the bytes of a file, or from a file, to tokens with spans;
     @(tsee build-token-trees-from-bytes)
     and @(tsee build-token-trees-from-file) go on to token trees.")
   (xdoc::h3 "Which files")
   (xdoc::p
    "rustc 1.87.0 does the four steps for
     the root file of a crate,
     for the file of a module,
     and for a file that the macro @('include!') names.
     For the macro @('include_str!')
     it only checks that the file is valid UTF-8,
     and the string has the byte order mark and the carriage returns;
     for @('include_bytes!') there is no step at all.")
   (xdoc::p
    "rustc 1.100.0 differs in two things.
     When @('include!') is used as an expression or as a statement,
     it does not skip a shebang in the file,
     whose first line is then lexed like the others.
     Since a shebang is not removed from a source text,
     the source text of a file is the same for both versions,
     and for every use of the file.")
   (xdoc::p
    "And where rustc 1.100.0 skips a shebang, it also looks for a frontmatter:
     lines between two lines of dashes, at the start of the file
     or after the shebang.
     That feature is unstable in rustc 1.100.0,
     which rejects a file that has one.
     In rustc 1.87.0 the dashes are tokens,
     and these are files of items, which do not start with a dash,
     so it rejects such a file as well.
     We follow rustc 1.87.0.")
   (xdoc::h3 "Differences from the Reference")
   (xdoc::p
    "There are two, both about the shebang;
     the first is also in @(see reference-vs-rustc).")
   (xdoc::ul
    (xdoc::li
     "The Reference says that a @('#!') does not start a shebang
      when it is followed by an opening square bracket,
      ignoring intervening comments or whitespace.
      rustc ignores only the comments that are not doc comments:
      @('#!/** d */[allow(unused)]') is a shebang.
      We do as rustc does.")
    (xdoc::li
     "The Reference removes the shebang
      up to and including the first line feed.
      In rustc the line feed is not part of the shebang;
      nor is a carriage return that immediately precedes the line feed
      (there can be one after the third step,
      if the file has two carriage returns there).
      Both are whitespace, so this makes no difference to the tokens,
      and no result of a compilation shows it:
      we have it from the source of rustc 1.87.0
      (the function @('strip_shebang') of its lexer).
      It makes a difference to line numbers:
      with the line feed in place,
      the lines of the text that is lexed are the lines of the file.
      We do as rustc does."))
   (xdoc::p
    "The third step is done once:
     a carriage return before a carriage return and a line feed stays.
     The Reference does not say whether it does;
     the Reference for Rust 1.100.0 says so
     (see @(see reference-vs-rustc))."))
  :order-subtopics t
  :default-parent t)

